</svg></div><div class="side"><div class="readouts"><div class="readout"><div class="name" id="kwscore-name">Keyword share of score (thousandths)</div><output id="kwscore" data-synapse-readout="kwscore" aria-labelledby="kwscore-name"></output></div><div class="readout"><div class="name" id="vecscore-name">Vector share of score (thousandths)</div><output id="vecscore" data-synapse-readout="vecscore" aria-labelledby="vecscore-name"></output></div><div class="readout"><div class="name" id="fused-name">Fused score (thousandths)</div><output id="fused" data-synapse-readout="fused" aria-labelledby="fused-name"></output></div></div><p id="found" class="card found" aria-live="polite"></p><details class="card fine"><summary>Keyboard controls</summary><div class="row"><label for="kwrank">Keyword rank</label><input type="range" id="kwrank" data-synapse-control="kwrank" min="1" max="20" step="1" value="1" data-unit="place" aria-valuetext="1 place"></div><div class="row"><label for="vecrank">Vector rank</label><input type="range" id="vecrank" data-synapse-control="vecrank" min="1" max="20" step="1" value="1" data-unit="place" aria-valuetext="1 place"></div></details></div></section><section class="card hints"><h2>Need a hint?</h2><details><summary>Hint 1</summary><p>The score 1/(k + rank) falls as the rank number grows, but it falls fastest near the front of the list (rank 1 to rank 3).</p></details><details><summary>Hint 2</summary><p>A document that is fairly good in both lists can beat one that is excellent in one list and poor in the other, because the two scores are added.</p></details></section><section class="explain"><h2>Why it happens</h2><p>Reciprocal rank fusion ignores the raw scores of each search method, which are on different scales, and uses only the rank position. Each list gives the document 1/(k + rank). A document at the front of a list gets a large share, and one far down gets a small share. The two shares are added to make one fused score, and the documents are then sorted by that score to form the single merged list.</p><p>The constant k controls how steeply the shares fall. A small k makes the first few places count much more than later ones. A large k, such as 60, makes the shares more even, so being found by both searches matters more than winning one list. A document missing from one list simply gets no share from it.</p></section></main><script>
